                "result": {
                  "schemaVersion": "operator-studio.agent-result/v1",
                  "format": "text-fallback",
                  "summary": "{\"schemaVersion\":\"operator-studio.agent-result/v1\",\"summary\":\"Rewrote run(inputs) in workspace run.py to cut eager dispatches from 6 to 4 (mul, alpha-FMA add, in-place sub, in-place relu) and remove the redundant .clone(); arithmetic is unchanged (1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125 -> ReLU), inputs are never mutated, and all frozen functions (inputs_for/get_inputs/get_test_cases/get_benchmark_inputs/reference) are byte-identical to the baseline.\",\"diagnosis\":{\"summary\":\"The operator is dispatch/launch-bound, not compute-bound: N=8192 FP32 (32 KiB/tensor) needs ~8 blocks of work, so DRAM time is ~10^2 ns while the measured baseline of 15.36 us over 6 eager dispatches is ~2.56 us per dispatch. The only lever with real physical yield is reducing the number of dispatched kernels; the goal of 99.9999% (1e6x) is unreachable for this operator, since an empty Python call plus one empty CUDA launch already exceeds 1e-6 x baseline for any plausible baseline in the 10-100 us range. Direction answer: switch away from '3-kernel alpha-folding' as previously formulated, because candidate-03 was not merely in the same family - it was mathematically wrong. torch.add(x, y, alpha=0.6) then out.add_(-0.1, alpha=1.25) yields x + 0.6*y - 0.125, which drops the required 1.25 factor on x; the correct target is 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125. That candidate could never pass 4/4 correctness regardless of the runner termination. The actionable conclusion is therefore not 'abandon dispatch reduction' but 'do dispatch reduction with correct math and a genuinely smaller op count'. Single-launch fusion via torch.compile / Triton / custom CUDA is not available under the pytorch-python contract and cannot be validated without local GPU runs, so this round takes the largest safe dispatch reduction reachable in pure eager PyTorch.\",\"bottlenecks\":[\"Per-call CPU dispatch cost (~2.5 us per eager aten op) dominates; 6 dispatches in baseline -> 15.36 us p50.\",\"Two scalar muls + one tensor add + one scalar sub + relu + clone are all separately dispatched; the clone is pure overhead (extra kernel + extra 32 KiB allocation).\",\"Current design-flaw: any 'single launch' path (torch.compile inductor, Triton, custom CUDA) is out of contract for pytorch-python and untestable locally; next best is minimizing eager dispatch count with a guarded, semantically identical op chain.\",\"Metric reachability: 99.9999% improvement implies new p50 <= 1e-6 x baseline, below the cost of a single empty CUDA launch; only input-keyed memoization/output reuse could 'reach' it, and that would fail 4/4 freshness-based correctness. This is a metric-definition conflict to escalate at mission level, not something to chase with more candidates.\"},\"patch\":\"diff --git a/run.py b/run.py\\n--- a/run.py\\n+++ b/run.py\\n@@ -24,4 +24,11 @@\\n def run(inputs):\\n-    # Initial implementation deliberately does one redundant device copy.\\n-    value = torch.relu(inputs['x'] * 1.25 + inputs['y'] * 0.75 - 0.125)\\n-    return value.clone()\\n+    # Baseline issued 6 eager dispatches (mul, mul, add, sub, relu, clone).\\n+    # Here the second scalar mul is folded into an alpha-scaled FMA add, the\\n+    # scalar shift and the ReLU are applied in place on the freshly allocated\\n+    # result, and the redundant .clone() is dropped: 4 dispatches and one\\n+    # allocation instead of 6 dispatches and two allocations.\\n+    # Math is unchanged: 1.25*x + 0.75*y - 0.125, then ReLU.\\n+    # Neither input tensor is ever written to.\\n+    value = torch.mul(inputs['x'], 1.25)\\n+    value.add_(inputs['y'], alpha=0.75)\\n+    value.sub_(0.125)\\n+    return value.relu_()\\n\",\"candidates\":[{\"id\":\"candidate-01\",\"title\":\"4-dispatch correct affine: fold 0.75*y into FMA alpha-add, in-place shift+relu, drop redundant clone\",\"hypothesis\":\"Baseline p50 is dispatch-bound at ~2.56 us per aten op (15.36 us / 6 ops). Dropping the clone and collapsing mul+mul/add/sub into mul + alpha-add + sub_ + relu_ removes 2 of 6 dispatches and one 32 KiB allocation, predicting p50 ~10 us (~1.5x) on the primary profile, with the same relative gain on the small profile.\",\"change\":\"run(inputs) now does: value = torch.mul(inputs['x'], 1.25); value.add_(inputs['y'], alpha=0.75); value.sub_(0.125); return value.relu_(). All other functions are untouched and identical to the baseline, including reference(inputs) which still computes torch.relu(x*1.25 + y*0.75 - 0.125). No module-level cache, no input mutation, no shape/stride/dtype assumptions beyond the contract.\",\"files\":[\"run.py\"],\"sourceReferences\":[],\"risks\":[\"FMA rounding in add_(alpha=0.75) can differ from the baseline's separate 0.75*y then add by ~1 ulp; well inside atol=1e-5 / rtol=1e-5 for values in [0, ~9.9].\",\"In-place chain is applied only to the freshly allocated result of torch.mul, so inputs['x'] and inputs['y'] are never written and repeated calls with the same input dict stay deterministic.\",\"Previous round's candidate-03 died with SHARED_GPU_RUNNER_FAILED (no result record); this diff uses only the most vanilla aten ops (mul, add_ with alpha, sub_ with Scalar, relu_) to keep the fast path free of anything that could abort a runner.\",\"Improvement will be ~1.5x, not the requested 1e6x; the stated target is physically unreachable for N=8192 and should be escalated as a metric-definition conflict rather than pursued with semantic shortcuts.\"]}],\"recommendedCandidate\":\"candidate-01\",\"nextAction\":{\"type\":\"candidate.plan\",\"title\":\"Queue candidate-01 for correctness + full benchmark on local-shared-gpu\",\"reason\":\"This is the smallest correct dispatch-reducing diff available under the pytorch-python contract and is deliberately minimal so that the shared-GPU queue either produces a clean record (proving the pipeline works) or isolates the failure to infrastructure rather than to candidate semantics.\",\"expectedOutput\":\"4/4 correctness pass and primary-profile p50 around 9-11 us versus the 15.36 us naive_v0 baseline (~1.4-1.7x), plus a recorded result row that distinguishes infra failure from candidate failure.\",\"risk\":\"low\"},\"risks\":[\"Round-2 context shows the previous candidate ended in SHARED_GPU_RUNNER_FAILED with no result record; if that recurs, the fault is in the runner/timing path, not in the operator implementation, and further candidate churn would be wasted queue budget.\",\"The stated 99.9999% target is not achievable for this shape (dispatch floor > 1e-6 x baseline); no legitimate candidate will satisfy the accept gate, so the mission should treat the improvement as directional evidence and escalate the metric definition.\",\"Deliberately not attempted this round: torch.compile / Triton single-kernel fusion (out of pytorch-python contract, unverifiable locally, and inductor's per-call guard+launcher overhead is likely comparable to or larger than 4 eager dispatches at this size) and CUDA-graph replay keyed on input pointers (higher complexity, returns a reused output buffer, and risks overwriting a previously returned output held by the harness).\"]}",
